      "message": "Thank you for the feedback — it was very helpful, and we\u0027ve reworked our design along the lines you suggested.\n\nBlur control API. We\u0027re dropping the shape-specific factories (gradient/circle with percent/pivot parameters) entirely. The filter will instead take a min and max blur sigma plus an SkShader whose alpha channel interpolates between them per pixel, exactly as you described. Shape logic (linear, radial, or anything else) moves out of the filter and into the mask shader supplied by the caller. We\u0027re deferring the SkImageFilter-mask variant for now, given the extra intermediate image it requires.\n\nReusing rescale(). We\u0027re rebuilding the downsample chain on skif::FilterResult::rescale(), invoking it once per level with a 0.5 scale factor so each mip level inherits the tile-mode boundary handling (we previously decimated with decal everywhere, which darkened clamp/repeat edges). The upsample side stays custom: since the retained mip levels are the blur and get blended per pixel by LOD, we walk back up 2x at a time with an 8-tap Kawase kernel to suppress grid artifacts — there\u0027s no analog for that in rescale()\u0027s single deferred upscale.\n\nOne follow-up thought: rescale()\u0027s per-step filtering is bilinear, while our chain previously used a small tent kernel. If rescale() ever grew an option for the per-step sampling kernel, it would serve both this filter and the uniform dual-Kawase SkBlurEngine algorithm you mentioned — we\u0027d be happy to align on that, since our downsample/upsample passes are essentially the dual-Kawase structure with levels retained.\n\nWe\u0027ll start with an internal refactor (mask-driven single pyramid-blend path), then switch the decimation to rescale() as a second step, and share results.",
